## 2. WHY QUARKUS? — PROBLEMS IT SOLVES

### Problems with Traditional Java (Spring Boot, Jakarta EE)

| Problem | Traditional Java | Quarkus |
|---------|-----------------|---------|
| Startup time | 5–30 seconds | 0.015s (native), ~1s (JVM) |
| Memory usage | 200–500 MB | 12–50 MB (native), 70–150 MB (JVM) |
| Container size | 200+ MB | 20–50 MB (native) |
| Cloud costs | High (always running) | Low (fast scale-up, serverless-ready) |
| Dev feedback | Restart on change | Live reload (instant) |
| Build-time optimization | None (all at runtime) | Maximum work at build time |
| Kubernetes readiness | Needs extra config | Built-in, first-class |

### When to Use Quarkus
- **Microservices** — fast startup is critical
- **Serverless / FaaS** — cold start matters
- **Kubernetes deployments** — container-optimized
- **Event-driven / Reactive** — built-in Vert.x + Mutiny
- **High-density deployments** — low memory = more instances
- **Cloud-native** — AWS Lambda, Azure Functions, Google Cloud Run

### When NOT to Use Quarkus
- Large monolithic enterprise apps already on Spring (migration cost too high)
- Extreme ecosystem dependency on Spring-only libraries
- Team has no Java experience (consider Go, Node.js instead)

